{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795714488,"nonce":"zbaBZcDc6uCa/b8AMLnG/bdM5tY=","title":"CI never runs the cuDSS-dependent half of the direct-solver dispatch: a CPU test pattern with a forced backend choice","message":"**What goes wrong.** Which factorization a direct solve uses depends on `jno.solve._preferred_direct_backend()`, which returns `\"cudss\"` only on a GPU, in float64, with the cuDSS stack importable. CI runs on CPU, so it always gets `\"device\"`, and everything that differs under cuDSS is untested there: whether a call site asks the preferred backend at all, the complex and symmetric LDL^T branch (`solver_context`), the residual gate, the plan caches. Several recent bugs were found only by hand on a GPU: #391 and #220 (a site hard-wires `sparse_lu_solve`), #383 (seven cuDSS failures), #385.\n\n**Reproduce.** On CPU, force the choice and spy on the solvers. Measured on main with `_preferred_direct_backend` patched to return `\"host\"`, a 1-D Poisson `fem.solve()` with no `linear=` calls `sparse_lu_solve` once and `host_lu_solve` never; with `linear=jno.solve.lu()` it calls `host_lu_solve` once. A site that ignores the preferred backend is visible on CPU.\n\n**Expected.** A test fixture that makes the dispatch observable without a GPU, so that \"this site ignores lu()'s choice\" fails in CI.\n\n**Where.** tests/test_default_direct_solve.py already patches the choice in `test_lu_picks_its_backend_where_the_solve_runs`; the pattern is not applied to the solve paths themselves.\n\n**Direction.**\n1. A fixture `forced_direct_backend(name)` that monkeypatches `jno.solve._preferred_direct_backend` to a CPU-capable backend (`\"host\"`, and `\"pardiso\"` where installed) and counts calls to the `linear.*_lu_solve` functions. Parametrise a small scenario set over it: steady linear, complex, 1-D, periodic, Hermite, `newton(direct=True)`, `staggered(direct=True)`, a transient step. Assert the solution is unchanged and that the named backend was called and `sparse_lu_solve` was not.\n2. For the cuDSS-only logic (symmetry detection, the complex branch, the residual gate), a fake `\"cudss\"`: patch `_cudss_available` to True and `cudss_lu_solve` / `cudss_ldl_solve` with SuperLU-backed stand-ins of the same contract, so the dispatch runs on CPU. The kernels stay covered by the GPU run.\n\nNeither is implemented here.","files":null}]}